{"maxId":206,"entries":{"chapter-53-study-hip-fractures|Chapter 53 Study: Hip Fractures":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"definition-amp-epidemiology|Definition & epidemiology":["u3"],"copy|Osteoporosis and propensity to fall are prominent risks. Average age is 82; approximately 18% of women and 36% of men die within the first postfracture year. (p. 793)":["u4"],"diagnosis-amp-repair|Diagnosis & repair":["u5"],"copy|Painful, shortened, externally rotated leg is typical; nondisplaced fractures may still permit walking. Persistent suspicion with negative radiographs → MRI, more sensitive than CT. (p. 794)":["u6"],"copy|Displaced femoral-neck fractures threaten the head's blood supply and are usually treated with arthroplasty; intertrochanteric fractures have better blood supply and generally heal with internal fixation. (p. 794)":["u7"],"management-amp-recovery|Management & recovery":["u8"],"copy|Reconcile medications and individualize surgical decisions using prior function, cognition, comorbidities and goals. Nonsurgical care may suit selected patients with very high operative risk or very short life expectancy. (p. 797)":["u9"],"copy|Mobilize on the day of surgery if possible, certainly by postoperative day 1; remove an inserted catheter the following morning. Monitor delirium, pulmonary complications and heart failure. (p. 797)":["u10"],"copy|Match rehabilitation to functional loss, endurance, ability to participate and home support. US Medicare inpatient rehabilitation generally requires 3 hours/day on at least 5 days/week, or 15 hours within 7 consecutive days in documented cases. At least two therapy disciplines are needed, including PT or OT (p800; CMS inpatient rehabilitation criteria).":["u11"],"copy|Hazzard gives 21–31 days of postoperative thromboprophylaxis; NICE recommends one month after fragility hip/proximal femur fracture when VTE risk outweighs bleeding risk. Up to 50% of previously independent older adults may need walking aids at one year (p801; NICE NG89, recommendation 1.11.2).":["u12"],"drugs-amp-prevention|Drugs & prevention":["u13"],"copy|Individualize analgesia for sustained relief and therapy-related pain while minimizing sedation; the optimal regimen is not established here. A preoperative nerve block can reduce pain. (p. 797)":["u14"],"copy|Replete vitamin D before starting a bisphosphonate; check the level during admission if not measured recently. (p. 798)":["u15"],"exam-traps|Exam traps":["u16"],"copy|Walking does not exclude a nondisplaced fracture. (p. 794)":["u17"],"copy|General versus spinal anesthesia: no mortality difference was found. (p. 797)":["u18"],"copy|Slow recovery warrants assessment for persistent delirium, depression, medication burden, nutritional deficiencies and isolation. (p. 800)":["u19"],"recall|Recall":["u20"],"past-paper-questions-on-this-chapter|Past-paper questions on this chapter":["u21"],"figure-53-5-book-image|Figure 53-5 — book image":["u27"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, Figure 53-5, p.800":["u28","u48"],"copy|CHAPTER 53s / HAZZARD":["u29"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 53; the book is the reference.":["u30"],"copy|Full chapter available: Read Chapter 53.":["u31"],"copy|Fast build from the book; not lane-reviewed.":["u32"],"copy|1.":["u33"],"copy|Which two underlying risks are emphasized for hip fracture?":["u34"],"copy|2.":["u35"],"copy|How do the approximate first-year mortality estimates differ by sex?":["u36"],"copy|7.":["u37"],"copy|Is nonsurgical care excluded for every hip fracture?":["u38"],"copy|9.":["u39"],"copy|When should mobilization and catheter removal occur?":["u40"],"copy|12.":["u41"],"copy|What nonmedical factors can determine safe discharge home?":["u42"],"copy|13.":["u43"],"copy|What participation requirements distinguish acute inpatient rehabilitation?":["u44"],"copy|14.":["u45"],"copy|What should be investigated when recovery is slower than expected?":["u46"],"copy|Answer":["u49","u51","u57","u59","u63","u65","u69"],"copy|Osteoporosis and propensity to fall. (p. 793)":["u50"],"copy|Approximately":["u52"],"copy|18%":["u53"],"copy|of women and":["u54"],"copy|36%":["u55"],"copy|of men are expected to die during the first year. (p. 793)":["u56"],"copy|No. It may be considered for selected patients, including those with very high surgical risk, very short life expectancy, or advanced dementia with contractures; prior function and goals matter. (p. 797)":["u58"],"copy|Mobilize on the day of surgery if possible, certainly by postoperative day":["u60"],"copy|1":["u61"],"copy|; remove an inserted catheter the morning after surgery. (p. 797)":["u62"],"copy|Home layout, social support, caregiver availability and resources, alongside mobility and self-care ability; a home assessment by therapy staff supplies essential information. (p. 798)":["u64"],"copy|In a US Medicare inpatient rehabilitation facility, generally":["u66"],"copy|3 hours/day on at least 5 days/week":["u67"],"copy|, or":["u67"],"copy|15 hours in 7 consecutive days":["u67"],"copy|in documented cases. Multiple therapy disciplines are required, including PT or OT (p800; CMS inpatient rehabilitation criteria).":["u68"],"copy|Persistent delirium, depression, polypharmacy, vitamin D or other nutritional deficiencies, and social isolation. (p. 800)":["u70"],"copy|Hazzard 8e, Chapter 53, printed pages 793–804.":["u32"],"copy|Match rehabilitation to functional loss, endurance, ability to participate and home support. Hazzard requires the ability to participate in at least 3 hours of therapy per day and input from at least two rehabilitation disciplines, such as PT and OT (p800).Current practice (not the book): CMS generally requires intensive therapy 3 hours/day on at least 5 days/week or, in documented cases, 15 hours within 7 consecutive days, with multiple disciplines including PT or OT. CMS inpatient rehabilitation criteria.":["u11"],"copy|Hazzard gives 21–31 days of postoperative thromboprophylaxis. Up to 50% of previously independent older adults may need walking aids at one year (p801).Current practice (not the book): NICE recommends one month after fragility hip/proximal femur fracture when VTE risk outweighs bleeding risk. NICE NG89, recommendation 1.11.2.":["u12"],"copy|A subtrochanteric break lies within 5 cm below the lesser trochanter, and the displaced fragments in 2020 Q57 require a nail spanning the break (p794 and the 2017 hip-fracture review, Figures 2–3 and pp.":["u106"],"copy|Hazzard says total replacement is thought to give better long-term results and less pain for active patients (p794).":["u199"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): In the later HEALTH trial of previously ambulatory patients with displaced neck fractures, the average functional advantage at two years was clinically small.":["u112"],"copy|Consider the person's usual activity and preferences when discussing this trade-off.":["u114"],"copy|Hazzard found no mortality difference between spinal and general anesthesia and describes peripheral nerve block for pain.":["u200"],"copy|The printed exam locator p869 concerns dementia; hip-fracture anesthesia is on p797.":["u137"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): Spinal anesthesia did not improve survival or walking recovery in REGAIN and delirium rates were similar.":["u134"],"copy|ASRA bases neuraxial timing on apixaban dose and elapsed time; one fixed two-day interval does not cover every patient.":["u201"],"copy|REGAIN · ASRA fifth edition, apixaban recommendations.":["u138"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): NICE advises one month of prophylaxis after fragility hip/proximal femur fracture when thrombotic risk outweighs bleeding risk.":["u202"],"copy|Hazzard requires the ability to participate in":["u203"],"copy|at least 3 hours of therapy per day":["u204"],"copy|and input from":["u205"],"copy|at least two rehabilitation disciplines":["u66"],"copy|, such as PT and OT (p800).":["u206"],"copy|CHAPTER 53 / HAZZARD":["u29"],"copy|June 2023 Q47 — mcq-78b3359272a717f584c23b2c":["u22"],"copy|May 2024 Q11 — mcq-a6c338a57828b765465f7cbf":["u23"],"copy|May 2024 Q17 — mcq-72e468cf141dda15964157c9":["u24"],"copy|May 2024 Q35 — mcq-d59bcda0cc381a35cc3134db":["u25"],"copy|June 2025 Q60 — mcq-10163f2b99f5abe875d7d917":["u26"],"copy|May 2024 Q17":["u47"]},"sequenceOffsets":[[207,-136],[242,-135],[246,-133],[247,-132],[263,-124],[285,-123],[322,0]]}
